#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# solve_always

#% text
# `solve_always(eqs, x)` gives the values of the other symbols for which the equations hold for every value of the variables x (a symbol or a list): the solutions of the system that sets every coefficient of the equations, as polynomials in x, to zero.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The equations are polynomial or rational in the variables; a rational equation counts through the numerator of lhs - rhs after `together`. The solutions are those of `solve`, as a list of lists of rules. When there are more symbols than coefficient equations, the first symbols in the canonical order are solved for in terms of the others.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
solve_always(1 + 2*x + 3*x^2 == a + b*x + c*x^2, x)
#% output
#= [[a -> 1, b -> 2, c -> 3]]

#% cell
solve_always((a - 2*b + 1)*x^2 + (a - b^2 - c)*x == a^2 - b + 3*c - 1, x)
#% output
#= [[a -> -2 - 13^(1/2), b -> (1/2)*(-1 - 13^(1/2)), c -> (1/2)*(-11 - 3*13^(1/2))], [a -> -2 + 13^(1/2), b -> (1/2)*(-1 + 13^(1/2)), c -> (1/2)*(-11 + 3*13^(1/2))]]

#% text
# The coefficients of a cubic from its roots:

#% cell
solve_always(x^3 + a*x^2 + b*x + c == (x - r)*(x - s)*(x - t), x)
#% output
#= [[a -> -r - s - t, b -> r*s + r*t + s*t, c -> -r*s*t]]

#% text
# An identity in two variables:

#% cell
solve_always(a*x + b*y + c*x*y == 2*x - y, [x, y])
#% output
#= [[a -> 2, b -> -1, c -> 0]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `coefficient_rules`
